# Prefer `var` in Java

Use `var` for local variables when the initializer makes the type obvious.

```java
// ❌ BAD
BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(response, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
List<String> header = readHoldingsHeader(reader);
Set<String> tickers = new LinkedHashSet<>();

// ✅ GOOD
var reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(response, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
var header = readHoldingsHeader(reader);
var tickers = new LinkedHashSet<String>();
```

Keep an explicit type when `var` is not allowed (fields, method parameters, `catch`, or a variable assigned later in a `while ((line = ...) != null)` loop).
